# Standard library: logarithms
#
# The natural logarithm and the exponential function.
#
# One record per item. A record begins at column 0 with its kind, axiom,
# theorem or definition, and its name; `mundane` before the kind marks an
# item a proof takes for granted. A proof imports each item it cites,
# `import <header> stdlib/logarithms/<name>`, where the header is the
# record's own before its name, as `mundane theorem` or `axiom`. Format is described in DATABASE.md.
#
# An item's statement is written in the theorem form of SYNTAX.md: let and
# assume lines with labels, then a `then` line.
#
# Every item carries at least one of:
#   metamath    a set.mm label or labels supply it
#   open        it is cited but unproved and unbridged
#
# Other fields: target, note, and for a function, sort, builds, reads and
# decided.


mundane definition ln
  sort        number → number
  builds      _1 clog cfv
  reads       the natural logarithm of
  decided     real arithmetic
  metamath    df-log, the inverse of the exponential function
  note        set.mm's log is taken in ℂ, its value at a negative number having
              an imaginary part; real arithmetic takes it only of a positive
              rational, or one times a whole power of e (METHODS.md), where it
              is the logarithm a reader knows. As for cos, this definition
              states nothing but what ln(x) builds.

mundane definition exp
  sort        number → number
  builds      _1 ce cfv
  reads       the exponential of
  decided     real arithmetic
  metamath    df-ef, by its power series
  note        exp(x) is eˣ, which set.mm defines the other way round, e being
              exp(1) (df-e). Real arithmetic takes it of a whole number of at
              least 0 plus whole multiples of logarithms of primes.
